<<~ ahu #what-travels >>

! Federating a Vessel

A Lararium runs as a ''swarm of one operator's vessels'' (a PersonaGroup) — each device an equal peer, each holding the operator's bags, syncing as a causal island. This carrier walks how a //second// vessel (say, a QA-lab box) joins the swarm.

''What github carries'' (public, shared by clone):
* the source, and
* `genesis/island.bin` — the self-booting wiki-quine (engine + memes; no private identity).

''What NEVER enters github'' (identity-bearing — gitignored):
* `.lararium/.vessel-key-*.json` — the operator's private signing key.
* `genesis/social-bootstrap.json` — the PersonaGroup pointers (admin/identities/circles/sessions doc-URLs + the sentinel PersonaGroup / MeshCabal IDs).

These travel ''out-of-band'' (a secure channel you control), never the public remote. A clone alone cannot join a PersonaGroup; joining requires what the founding vessel hands over deliberately.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #two-models >>

!! Two Identity Models

> ''Model A is a TEMPORARY stopgap, not the target.'' Copying one key across vessels names the ''antipattern'' the multi-device literature warns against — SSB-fusion and Veilid both copy-the-key and both lose per-device revocation; Keybase built its per-device model precisely to escape "one PGP key everywhere." The pono target rides the ''5-scale model'': each vessel mints its OWN distinct key — the ''user×vessel bond'' (Plane 0) — delegated into the operator ''PersonaGroup'' (Plane 1) by a signed edge. The signed-delegation edge composes up the ''SOCIAL axis'' `device-vessel → PersonaGroup → CabalGroup` (who reads); above it, ''NexusGroup'' and ''DreamNet'' federate across the ''INFRA axis'' by treaty / mutual carriage-contract (who relays), not by delegation-up — the two axes cross at the vessel (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#two-axis-topology >>). The key is the node; the ''delegation is the relationship''. The identity model — the five scales, the per-vessel key, the delegation edge — stands whole at <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity >>; its mesh topology at <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture >>.

''Model A — one operator identity across devices (works today).'' Every vessel carries the //same// operator key, so every vessel presents the //same// DID — already a member of the PersonaGroup. The QA box receives the operator key and a device-admit payload out-of-band, loads them, and boots as the same operator on another device.

''Model B — a distinct key per device, delegated in (pending the sync layer).'' Each vessel forges its //own// key; the founder receives the joinee's contact-card and delegates that DID into the group (`runDeviceAdmitAccept` — built, founder-side correct). But a joining device needs ''two'' things to read the group, and only one reaches it from JS today:

# the ''key-chain + membership'' (CGKA + delegation ops) — JS-available via `eventsForAgent` / ingest; necessary but not sufficient; and
# the encrypted ''Document content'' itself — which Keyhive moves via ''sedimentree'' (content-addressed strata sync) under ''Beelay'' (the auth-sync RPC; zero-knowledge server). ''Both are Rust-only — not exposed in the `keyhive_wasm` JS surface'' (and the `automerge/beelay` repo is dead; the live code is `beelay-core` inside the keyhive monorepo).

So a fresh-key join still fails the boot membership gate because the Document never arrives. Closing it from JS means transporting the document ourselves — ship an `Archive` (`Archive.toBytes` → `ingestArchive`) or the ciphertext blobs over our own transport — or binding Beelay to WASM. The cross-peer admit + offline-device work is ''actively in-flight'' in the keyhive repo (PRs #205 prekey-archives / offline-admit, #116 doc-sync listener, #110 reachable-docs). The grain (operator ruling 2026-06-23): ''roll our OWN temporary Model B'' rather than wait on the pre-alpha Rust branch. A distinct per-vessel key, admitted by a signed delegation edge, with an ''envelope key-handoff'' (libsodium sealed-box) so the joinee decrypts WITHOUT Beelay — behind the four-port swap surface so Keyhive / BeeKEM / Beelay slot in later with low churn. That keying-and-swap model stands stated whole at <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity#four-port-swap >> (and #encrypt-from-start); here it carries only the federation consequence. <<~ confidence Provisional-Synthesis 7/20 >> Model A holds only until Model B lands. Track under <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/lares/mesh-governance >>.

The procedure below enacts ''Model A'' (the current stopgap).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #three-axes >>

!! Three Axes ~ one inversion of control

The SOCIAL/INFRA cut names two axes; a third rides beside them, and the three answer three orthogonal
questions. ''INFRA'' asks //who RELAYS// (Nexus / DreamNet, by treaty / carriage-contract — route through,
never trust). ''SOCIAL'' asks //who READS// (`vessel → PersonaGroup → CabalGroup`, by signed Keyhive
delegation — boolean, present-tense, revocable). ''REPUTATION'' asks //how much you WEIGH// (the vouch-DAG,
by a seed-rooted ε-fold — scalar, accumulated, sybil-bounded; <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity#the-lineage-fold >>).

The three never collapse into one because no axis derives another: a member READS a cabal (holds the cap)
yet WEIGHS little (a shallow lineage), or weighs heavily yet sits revoked. Authorization runs
forgery-resistant (a delegation never forges); reputation runs sybil-resistant (standing never forges,
because minting keys splits mass — the bottleneck property). Different threat models, different math,
different temporal grain.

''SOCIAL splits again — self ⊥ org — and each admission runs its own flow.'' The who-reads chain
`vessel → PersonaGroup → CabalGroup` folds TWO concerns the gate keeps apart. ''SOCIAL/self'' — a vessel
joining its own human's PersonaGroup — runs SINGLE-principal: you admit your own device, so no vouch, no
price, only a delegation and the sealed document handoff (the crossing). ''ORG'' — a member joining a
shared CabalGroup — runs MULTI-principal and carries MULTIPLICITY: one member joins many cabals, each its
own Circle with its own founders, its own invite policy, its own vouch-lineage, so your weight in cabal A
never transfers to cabal B. Three admission flows fall out, and they stay distinct: ''(1) vessel →''
''PersonaGroup'' (self, no vouch — the envelope handoff); ''(2) user → DreamNet'' (the outer gate,
invite-only-at-first, vouch-seeded at the mesh root); ''(3) member → cabal(s)'' (per-cabal, multiplicity).
Flows 2 and 3 ride the SAME instruments (`cabal-invite` + `admission-price` + `vouch-dag`) and differ ONLY
by SCOPE — the `placeDocId` and the lineage `seed` — so the machinery composes across scales without the
flows collapsing into one. Flow 1 stands apart: a distinct mechanism (delegation + envelope), never the
vouch machinery.

''The through-line — one move made three times: control inverts to the edge, never a root.'' A Circle "holds
what mutual bindings declare" (the Kowloon / group-as-closure model, <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle#composition >>):
no root grants membership (the SOCIAL graph stands up bottom-up from the vessels' own signed edges), no
authority assigns standing (the REPUTATION graph folds from the vouches the peers themselves signed), no
relay owns you (the INFRA graph rides mutual carriage-treaty). ''The key is the node; the delegation is the
relationship.'' The ''Vault'' seals the inversion at the top — the collector that knows "these handles are
one human" stays the user's private secret, never a published graph, riding no wire
(<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle#the-vault >>).

''Two acts, not one — the vouch stays separable.'' A ''CabalGroup'' reads as a multi-principal Circle
(several humans' PersonaGroups mutually bound). Joining it fires an AUTHORIZATION act — a Keyhive
delegation, //"this vessel may now read the cabal."// A VOUCH — //"I stake my standing that this peer//
//belongs"// — rides as a SEPARATE act, and the two never fuse: a member you let read may carry no stake, a
peer you stake on may hold no cap. PGP keeps exactly this split — key-validity (public, exported) in one
store, owner-trust (private, never exported) in another, and one signs many keys at owner-trust zero.
Folding "I delegated to you" into "I vouch for you" names the confused-deputy bug (Miller, ocap), never a
feature. So the REPUTATION fold (`lineage-rank`) never derives its edges from the delegation log — it folds
the EXPLICIT vouches (the `CabalInvite` stake) alone. Storage MAY still share: a vouch MAY ride a delegation
record as an optional stake weight, but only as a separable dimension defaulting OFF, so access stands at
vouch=0 and the reputation read never shadows every grant. Both still invert to the user — the peers sign
their own edges, and no registry holds them.

<<~ confidence Synthesis-Canon 14/20 >> The inversion runs pure but for ONE seam: the multi-principal
key-handover inside a CabalGroup. A single-principal me-Circle (your own devices) needs no tie-break — you
hold the one ordering authority. A CabalGroup of several humans constitutes the smallest ordering seat for
itself (an elected kahu, or accept-and-heal) — the one place a Circle stands up a thin constitutional core,
and it stays the operator's seat (<<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle#composition >>).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #procedure >>

!! The Procedure (Model A, witnessed 2026-06-21)

''On the founding vessel'' — mint a clean admit payload (sentinel IDs + the group's signed cap-events):

```
lares device-admit --out admit.json
```

Transmit ''out-of-band'' to the new vessel: `admit.json`, and the operator key (`.lararium/.vessel-key-*.json`). Never the public remote.

''On the new vessel'' — bootstrap the CLI once (it cannot build itself into existence), place the operator key under `<LAR_ROOT>/.lararium/`, then stand up and join:

```
pnpm install && pnpm -r build      # bootstrap the lares CLI + node
lares wake --admit admit.json      # idempotent join-standup
```

`lares wake --admit` runs the same found-if-absent standup as `--install` — build, mempalace, genesis (skipped; `island.bin` rides the clone) — but the init step ''joins'' the PersonaGroup from the payload instead of founding a new one: it loads the operator key (same DID = a member), applies the cap-events, writes the local `social-bootstrap.json`, and stands the node up. The keyhive boot gates then verify membership and the vessel goes live. Re-running is a no-op (the bootstrap already lives).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #the-gate >>

!! The Gate, Honestly

A joining vessel proves itself on every boot through three keyhive gates: its DID matches its key (A), its Individual is a member of the PersonaGroup (B), and the PersonaGroup is a member of the Nexus MeshCabal (C). Under Model A all three pass because the vessel //is// the same operator. Under Model B, gate B fails until the new key is delegated in — which is why Model B waits on the contact-card exchange.

A vessel never joins by pulling a repo; it joins by receiving — deliberately, out-of-band — what the founder hands it. The public clone carries the house; the operator carries the key.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #windows >>

!! The Windows Surface

A QA vessel on native Windows 11 (PowerShell, not WSL) stands up through the same `lares wake --install` / `--admit`, with these surface notes:

* ''`lares` global'' — `--install`/`--admit` writes a `lares.cmd` shim beside the bin (on Unix it symlinks into `~/.local/bin`). Add that directory to PATH so `lares` runs bare. The link step is self-contained — it does not depend on `pnpm setup`/`PNPM_HOME`.
* ''Python'' — the integration check resolves `python3` → `python` → `py`, so Windows' `python`/`py` is found.
* ''The wake hook is portable'' — it runs node exec-form (`node lares-wake-hook.mjs`), no shell, identical on Windows and Unix.
* ''mempalace's keep-hooks are bash'' (`Stop` / `PreCompact`) — on native Windows they need ''Git for Windows'' (which Claude Code already requires for its bash tool). With Git Bash present, `lares wake --claude` wires them as `bash "<path>"`; without it, the verbatim keep-leg stays dark until Git for Windows is installed.
* ''`lares reconcile`'' (the dev restart) uses `lsof`/`ss` — Unix-only; it does not run during a normal wake, but the restart loop awaits a Windows (netstat/taskkill) path.

